# A1/A1.2/A1_11_more_about_yourself.letz
# Source: PDF slide deck "L2 a1convlotsmanmeli.pdf" (A1 Conversation course, Lesson 2)
# Gender markers (m)/(f)/(n) stripped; (formal)/(informal) qualifiers stripped.
# Words already taught in A1.01-A1.09 (al, d'Stad, kënnen, wunnen, fueren,
# schaffen, schwätzen) are reused in sentences below but not re-declared here.
# Fills added: language proficiency levels (fléissend/relativ gutt/e bëssen/keen),
# prepositions for countries and cities (zu/an/an der), keng/kee negation,
# mee (but) conjunction, and zanter + time expressions.
# Mined from deck grammar tables and exercises p.5-9, p.12-15.

@lesson A1.11 "More About Yourself"

# --- Age, marital & relationship status ---
@word den Alter = the age
@word jonk = young
@word Single = single

# --- Places ---
@word de Wunnuert = the place of residence
@word den Aarbechtsuert = the work place
@word d'Land = the country
@word de Bierg = the mountain
@word Däitschland = Germany
@word Berlin = Berlin
@word Sandweiler = Sandweiler

# --- Languages ---
@word Franséisch = French
@word Däitsch = German
@word fléissend = fluently

# --- Professions ---
@word de Beruff = the profession
@word den Dokter = the doctor
@word d'Doktesch = the doctor
@word de Proff = the secondary school teacher
@word de Schoulmeeschter = the primary school teacher
@word d'Schouljoffer = the primary school teacher (female)

# --- Family & pets ---
@word d'Duechter = the daughter
@word d'Duechteren = the daughters
@word d'Kaz = the cat
@word d'Kazen = the cats
@word de Mupp = the pooch
@word d'Muppen = the pooches

# --- Other useful words ---
@word keng = no, none
@word mee = but
@word laang = long
@word doheem = at home
@word geschwënn = soon

@sentence
@lu Ech si jonk.
@en I am young.
@distractor-en old
@distractor-en married
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu bestuet

@sentence
@lu Hien ass al.
@en He is old.
@distractor-en young
@distractor-en married
@distractor-lu jonk
@distractor-lu bestuet

@sentence
@lu Ech si Single.
@en I am single.
@distractor-en married
@distractor-en young
@distractor-lu bestuet
@distractor-lu jonk

@sentence
@lu Ech si bestuet.
@en I am married.
@distractor-en single
@distractor-en old
@distractor-lu Single
@distractor-lu al

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn doheem.
@en I am at home.
@distractor-en soon
@distractor-en long
@distractor-lu geschwënn
@distractor-lu laang

@sentence
@lu D'Duechter ass jonk.
@en The daughter is young.
@distractor-en old
@distractor-en married
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu bestuet

@sentence
@lu De Schoulmeeschter ass al.
@en The teacher is old.
@distractor-en friend
@distractor-en doctor
@distractor-lu Frënd
@distractor-lu Dokter

@sentence
@lu Hatt huet e Frënd.
@en She has a boyfriend.
@distractor-en girlfriend
@distractor-en husband
@distractor-lu Frëndin
@distractor-lu Mann

@sentence
@lu Meng Fra heescht Maria.
@en My wife is called Maria.
@distractor-en husband
@distractor-en son
@distractor-lu Mann
@distractor-lu Jong

@sentence
@lu Ech wunnen zu Lëtzebuerg.
@en I live in Luxembourg.
@distractor-en Belgium
@distractor-en Germany
@distractor-lu Belsch
@distractor-lu Däitschland

@sentence
@lu Ech fueren an d'Belsch.
@en I am driving to Belgium.
@distractor-en France
@distractor-en Luxembourg
@distractor-lu Frankräich
@distractor-lu Lëtzebuerg

@sentence
@lu Ech schaffen zu Sandweiler.
@en I work in Sandweiler.
@distractor-en Belgium
@distractor-en Germany
@distractor-lu Belsch
@distractor-lu Däitschland

@sentence
@lu Mir wunnen op d'Land.
@en We live in the countryside.
@distractor-en mountain
@distractor-en city
@distractor-lu Bierg
@distractor-lu Stad

@sentence
@lu D'Doktesch wunnt an der Stad.
@en The doctor lives in the city.
@distractor-en teacher
@distractor-en mountain
@distractor-lu Proff
@distractor-lu Bierg

@sentence
@lu D'Kaz ass op dem Bierg.
@en The cat is on the mountain.
@distractor-en dog
@distractor-en city
@distractor-lu Mupp
@distractor-lu Stad

@sentence
@lu Wéi ass Äre Familljennumm?
@en What is your surname?
@distractor-en forename
@distractor-en age
@distractor-lu Virnumm
@distractor-lu Alter

@sentence
@lu Wéi buschtawéiert een dat?
@en How does one spell that?
@distractor-en write
@distractor-en repeat
@distractor-lu schreiwen
@distractor-lu widderhuelen

@sentence
@lu Ech si Proff vu Beruff.
@en I am a teacher by profession.
@distractor-en doctor
@distractor-en salesman
@distractor-lu Dokter
@distractor-lu Verkeefer

@sentence
@lu Hie schwätzt fléissend Englesch.
@en He speaks English fluently.
@distractor-en she
@distractor-en French
@distractor-lu Hatt
@distractor-lu Franséisch

@sentence
@lu Ech schwätze relativ gutt Franséisch.
@en I speak French relatively well.
@distractor-en German
@distractor-en fluently
@distractor-lu Däitsch
@distractor-lu fléissend

@sentence
@lu Ech schwätzen e bëssen Däitsch.
@en I speak a little German.
@distractor-en French
@distractor-en fluently
@distractor-lu Franséisch
@distractor-lu fléissend

@sentence
@lu Ech kommen aus Däitschland, vu Berlin.
@en I come from Germany, from Berlin.
@distractor-en France
@distractor-en Belgium
@distractor-lu Frankräich
@distractor-lu Belsch

@sentence
@lu Mir hu keng Kanner, mee eng Kaz.
@en We have no children, but a cat.
@distractor-en dog
@distractor-en book
@distractor-lu Mupp
@distractor-lu Buch

@sentence
@lu Wéi laang wunnt Dir schonn zu Lëtzebuerg?
@en How long have you already been living in Luxembourg?
@distractor-en soon
@distractor-en Belgium
@distractor-lu geschwënn
@distractor-lu Belsch

@sentence
@lu Hatt schwätzt fléissend Franséisch a keen Englesch.
@en She speaks French fluently and no English.
@distractor-en German
@distractor-en well
@distractor-lu Däitsch
@distractor-lu gutt

@sentence
@lu D'Monika wunnt an Däitschland, zu München.
@en Monika lives in Germany, in Munich.
@distractor-en France
@distractor-en Luxembourg
@distractor-lu Frankräich
@distractor-lu Lëtzebuerg

@sentence
@lu De Steve schafft zu London.
@en Steve works in London.
@distractor-en Germany
@distractor-en Berlin
@distractor-lu Däitschland
@distractor-lu Berlin

# --- @fill: language proficiency levels ---------------------------------------
# The deck's four-step scale: fléissend / relativ gutt / e bëssen / keen.
# Distractors are the other levels — the four-way confusion.

@fill
@lu De Claudio schwätzt [fléissend] Italienesch.
@en Claudio speaks Italian [fluently].
@distractor-lu relativ gutt
@distractor-lu e bëssen
@distractor-lu keen
@distractor-en relatively well
@distractor-en a little
@distractor-en no

@fill
@lu Hatt schwätzt [relativ gutt] Lëtzebuergesch.
@en She speaks Luxembourgish [relatively well].
@distractor-lu fléissend
@distractor-lu e bëssen
@distractor-lu keen
@distractor-en fluently
@distractor-en a little
@distractor-en no

@fill
@lu Hatt schwätzt [e bëssen] Lëtzebuergesch.
@en She speaks [a little] Luxembourgish.
@distractor-lu fléissend
@distractor-lu relativ gutt
@distractor-lu keen
@distractor-en fluently
@distractor-en relatively well
@distractor-en no

@fill
@lu Ech schwätze [keen] Portugisesch.
@en I speak [no] Portuguese.
@distractor-lu fléissend
@distractor-lu relativ gutt
@distractor-lu e bëssen
@distractor-en fluently
@distractor-en relatively well
@distractor-en a little

# --- @fill: prepositions for countries and cities -----------------------------
# zu for Luxembourg and most towns; an for most countries;
# an der for countries with article (Belsch, Schwäiz). The three-way confusion.

@fill
@lu Hatt wunnt [zu] Lëtzebuerg.
@en She lives [in] Luxembourg.
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu op
@distractor-lu aus
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en on
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu D'Annette wunnt [zu] Clierf.
@en Annette lives [in] Clervaux.
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu op
@distractor-lu aus
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en on
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu Hie wunnt [an] Däitschland.
@en He lives [in] Germany.
@distractor-lu zu
@distractor-lu op
@distractor-lu aus
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en on
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu Mir wunne [an der] Belsch.
@en We live [in] Belgium.
@distractor-lu zu der
@distractor-lu op der
@distractor-lu aus der
@distractor-en at the
@distractor-en on the
@distractor-en from the

@fill
@lu Hatt fuert [an d'] Belsch.
@en She is driving [to] Belgium.
@distractor-lu an der
@distractor-lu op d'
@distractor-lu aus der
@distractor-en in the
@distractor-en on the
@distractor-en from the

# --- @fill: keng / kee negation -----------------------------------------------
# The gendered negative article: keen (masculine), keng (feminine/plural).
# Distractors swap the gender form.

@fill
@lu Hatt huet [keng] Geschwëster.
@en She has [no] siblings.
@distractor-lu keen
@distractor-lu net
@distractor-lu kee
@distractor-en not
@distractor-en any
@distractor-en some

@fill
@lu Hie schwätzt [keen] Franséisch.
@en He speaks [no] French.
@distractor-lu keng
@distractor-lu net
@distractor-lu kee
@distractor-en not
@distractor-en any
@distractor-en some

# --- @fill: mee (but) — joining contrasting clauses --------------------------
# Coordinating conjunction, distinct from subordinating "well" / "wann".

@fill
@lu Ech si jonk, [mee] ech schaffe schonn.
@en I am young, [but] I already work.
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu oder
@distractor-lu well
@distractor-en and
@distractor-en or
@distractor-en because

@fill
@lu Hie schwätzt Englesch, [mee] keen Däitsch.
@en He speaks English, [but] no German.
@distractor-lu an
@distractor-lu oder
@distractor-lu well
@distractor-en and
@distractor-en or
@distractor-en because

# --- @fill: zanter + time expressions ----------------------------------------
# "since / for (duration)" — the lesson already has the word and a sentence.

@fill
@lu Ech wunne [zanter] fënnef Joer zu Lëtzebuerg.
@en I have been living [for] five years in Luxembourg.
@distractor-lu bis
@distractor-lu vun
@distractor-lu säit
@distractor-en until
@distractor-en from
@distractor-en at

@fill
@lu Hatt schafft [zanter] dräi Joer als Doktesch.
@en She has been working [for] three years as a doctor.
@distractor-lu bis
@distractor-lu vun
@distractor-lu säit
@distractor-en until
@distractor-en from
@distractor-en at
